WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.500
A serverless function package is a supply chain boundary.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:15.400
It contains handler code, npm dependencies, configuration assumptions, build output, startup behavior, and sometimes shared layers or platform extensions.

3
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:24.780
A handler file may be short, but the deployed artifact can include hundreds of packages, generated bundles, native modules, and transitive dependencies.

4
00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:36.480
If the package contains stale libraries, unnecessary files, real environment files, private keys, or local test data, the platform will faithfully deploy those mistakes.

5
00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:40.680
Builds should be repeatable and easy to review.

6
00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:44.940
Use lockfiles and deterministic dependency installation where appropriate.

7
00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:54.560
Review changes to package.json, package-lock.json, bundler configuration, lifecycle scripts, generated artifacts, and shared layers.

8
00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:58.020
Keep deployment packages minimal, but not mysterious.

9
00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:05.560
Removing noise helps reduce attack surface and deployment time, but the package still needs everything required to run and troubleshoot safely.

10
00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:13.820
Avoid shipping source-control metadata, local caches, test fixtures, credentials, or development tooling unless there is a documented runtime reason.

11
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:20.740
Shared layers and internal packages can reduce duplication, but they also create shared responsibility.

12
00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:27.400
If ten functions depend on the same layer, a vulnerability or unsafe default in that layer affects ten functions.

13
00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:32.700
Updates need owners, versioning, testing, and rollout discipline.

14
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:39.600
The same is true for framework adapters, function wrappers, observability extensions, and bundlers.

15
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:45.060
A convenience package becomes part of the security posture once it is deployed into production functions.

16
00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:51.580
Runtime hygiene also includes keeping platform runtimes and Node.js versions current.

17
00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:57.280
Serverless teams sometimes forget older functions because they are quiet and rarely changed.

18
00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:59.540
Quiet is not the same as safe.

19
00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:04.680
Inventory deployed runtimes, package versions, layers, and build paths.

20
00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:12.860
A small function can carry a large dependency tree, so treat function packaging as production software delivery rather than a quick upload of code.
